  "configuration_id": "config_034",
  "reason": "Evidence: The dominant finding is that multiprofile provides a large gain (~8 pts) over singleprofile (config_005: 0.518 vs config_006: 0.435), and within multiprofile, avgpool+concat with scgpt+imagenet_vit_l is the current best. However, the RNA encoder axis is completely unexplored—we have only tested scgpt. Since RNA is the dominant signal (rna_only 0.411 vs image_only 0.227), the quality of the RNA representation is likely the next most impactful design lever. Question: Does switching the RNA encoder from scgpt to scvi_n200 (another learned, generative-style representation) improve or degrade performance in the multiprofile setting? config_034 (scvi_n200, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, concat, multiprofile) is a clean single-variable comparison to config_005, isolating the RNA encoder effect while holding all other design choices constant. Why best next experiment: It tests the highest-uncertainty, highest-impact unexplored axis (RNA representation) in the regime where performance is already highest, with a minimal change that cleanly attributes any performance shift to the encoder choice. The result will determine whether to invest further in learned RNA encoders or pivot to linear (PCA) representations, and will guide all subsequent experiments on the image encoder and fusion axes."
}

